&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.280
“This is the best way to institute an ethos,&nbsp;
because, at the end of the day, the thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.800
is always difficult about startups is early on&nbsp;
it's easy to move super fast when you're like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.480
six people in a room. You all can communicate&nbsp;
really easily et cetera. Once you become 600,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.520
all of a sudden decisions start to slow&nbsp;
down and things start to slow down.”
&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.120
It’s easy to look at Delian’s insane&nbsp;
level of energy and think he might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.040
getting ahead of himself on this whole&nbsp;
“manufacturing stuff in space” thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.920
but he’s been watching the industry closely for&nbsp;
years, and space manufacturing actually dates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:32.560
back to the sixties, so let’s go through&nbsp;
the history to see how we got here.
&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.440
“Our great system will be judged by&nbsp;
how we do in the field of space.”
&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.840
Even though America won the Space race&nbsp;
and landed a man on the moon on July 20th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:49.360
1969. It was the Russians who were technically&nbsp;
the first to manufacture something in space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.880
During the Soyuz 6 mission, Russian cosmonauts&nbsp;
welded aluminum, titanium, and stainless steel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.760
together using a hardware unit called Vulkan.
Four years later, the United States launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.640
Skylab, a fully functional space station&nbsp;
equipped with a materials processing facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.000
that could grow crystals, process alloys,&nbsp;
and weld metals using an electron beam.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.840
NASA astronauts spent 32 man-hours on materials&nbsp;
science and space manufacturing during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.880
mission and made some significant contributions&nbsp;
that would be rolled into future missions.
&nbsp;

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.880
Ten years after the Skylab launch, NASA began&nbsp;
using the Spacelab facility. Spacelab was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.960
module that was carried aboard the Space Shuttle&nbsp;
26 different times and served as a short-duration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.240
research platform. It wouldn’t stay in space, but&nbsp;
while it was up there it accomplished some pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.360
incredible things. Spacelab was an extremely&nbsp;
large program and could be reconfigured for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.400
each mission, which led to an extremely high&nbsp;
throughput of experimentation. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:54.160
in just one mission, NASA performed 73&nbsp;
separate experiments in materials science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:59.200
astrophysics, and atmospheric research. Here’s&nbsp;
a picture of a mercuric iodide crystal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.560
was grown on Spacelab 3. Pretty incredible.
Fast forward to today and essentially all space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.640
manufacturing takes place on the International&nbsp;
Space Station. Most of the products that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:14.320
made in space are designed to stay in space, and&nbsp;
this makes sense. Giving astronauts the ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.200
fabricate parts quickly while aboard the space&nbsp;
station speeds up research and development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:24.080
and there’s even a plan to 3D print food&nbsp;
on the station eventually, but Delian has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.320
a completely different vision for Varda. 
“The more commercial reason to start the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.320
is actually that there is a whole wide array&nbsp;
of products that could significantly improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.320
quality of life for people here on Earth that&nbsp;
can only be manufactured in microgravity.”
&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.120
Delian wants to bring everything that Varda&nbsp;
makes in space back down to earth. If that sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:47.360
insanely expensive, that’s because it is. At&nbsp;
least for now. Delian’s entire plan with Varda is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.320
to position the company to take advantage of the&nbsp;
exponentially decreasing cost of space travel.
&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.520
See, when it comes to making computer chips,&nbsp;
we’ve been blessed by exponentially increasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.240
capabilities for decades now. The number of&nbsp;
transistors in a dense integrated circuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.880
essentially doubles every two years. This&nbsp;
is called Moore’s Law and it explains why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.560
computers have been getting smaller,&nbsp;
faster, and cheaper so consistently.
&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.920
Something similar is happening with space&nbsp;
travel right now. When the space shuttle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:20.400
was in operation, launching a single kilogram into&nbsp;
space cost $54 thousand dollars on average. SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:26.080
has been able to reduce that cost to just under&nbsp;
$3 thousand dollars using their Falcon 9 rocket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.840
and it’s only getting cheaper.
Every year, SpaceX is launching more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:35.600
rockets and dropping the cost of getting to space.&nbsp;
This is critical to Delian’s plan for Varda and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.880
the main reason why he couldn’t pursue this idea&nbsp;
sooner. Elon plans to bring the cost per kilo down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:46.640
to just $10 dollars with Starship and while that’s&nbsp;
certainly an ambitious target, if he can even get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:50.880
close, the economics of space manufacturing&nbsp;
will start to make a lot of sense.
&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.160
But you’re probably asking yourself,&nbsp;
what would you even make in space?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:59.120
We seem to be doing fine here on Earth, so what&nbsp;
could possibly be worth all this effort. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:03.520
there are a few good examples:
The first is human organs. Growing a replacement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:08.160
heart or kidney in space might sound a little&nbsp;
weird to some people, but if you’re one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.280
one hundred thousand people waiting for an organ&nbsp;
transplant, I’m pretty sure you won't care where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:17.920
it came from, as long as it works. And there’s a&nbsp;
lot of evidence suggesting that growing organs in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:23.520
microgravity yields remarkable results, since the&nbsp;
cells won’t collapse under their own weight. 
&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.200
Semiconductor film could also benefit from&nbsp;
low-earth-orbit production, with some studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:33.520
estimating that quality could increase by&nbsp;
10 thousand percent. That’s a huge step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:38.080
up from what’s possible on earth, but that’s&nbsp;
because semiconductor film not only benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:43.440
from the microgravity environment, but also from&nbsp;
the complete lack of dust in space. Tiny dust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:47.360
particles routinely reduce the effectiveness&nbsp;
of semiconductors made here on earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:52.720
but that’s not a problem one thousand miles up.
The third opportunity is fiber-optic cables: which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.960
we use to move data around the globe every single&nbsp;
day. There is a special kind of fiber-optic cable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:02.400
made from fluoride glass called ZBLAN&nbsp;
that could have as little as one-tenth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:07.520
the signal loss of traditional optical&nbsp;
fiber. The problem is that these ZBLAN fibers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.760
are extremely difficult to make here on earth.&nbsp;
And, again, it all comes down to gravity.
&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:16.960
As the molten glass is stretched into ultrathin&nbsp;
fibers, tiny crystals tend to form, which can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:22.160
weaken signals. When you’re up in space, the&nbsp;
microgravity environment suppresses the formation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:26.960
of these crystals, so the resulting cables&nbsp;
can carry more data over longer distances. 
&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.440
Human tissue growth has been tested on&nbsp;
the ISS, but it’s impossible to even try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.520
fiber-optic production in space currently. 
“For example, in a lot of fiber optics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:38.720
there’s a toxic chemical that you want to coat&nbsp;
the fiber optic cable with that protects it at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:41.440
the end of the process. No f***ing way&nbsp;
is NASA going to let you put that toxic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.480
chemical on the ISS. If that thing gets out,&nbsp;
you’re potentially killing astronauts”
&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.880
But Varda’s space factories will be completely&nbsp;
autonomous. Everything Varda launches into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:52.960
space will be completely self contained,&nbsp;
meaning they can work on any manufacturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.280
problem without risking human lives. 
Okay, so we’ve established that the whole Varda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:02.480
project is only possible because scientists have&nbsp;
been testing space manufacturing for decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:06.960
and SpaceX has dropped the cost of rocket&nbsp;
launches so dramatically, but here’s where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:12.560
the technology gets really interesting.
Varda won’t be building rockets, only payloads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:19.040
that sit on top of SpaceX rockets, but they still&nbsp;
have to solve a few key problems once everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.520
is actually up there. So Delian and his team are&nbsp;
focusing on building three different systems.
&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:29.200
The first is the actual factory. This needs to be&nbsp;
small enough and light enough to fit on a rocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:33.360
while still holding all the raw materials&nbsp;
for whatever they are making on that trip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.000
but you can’t just shoot a box into space and&nbsp;
hope that it stays up there and then lands where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.520
you can find it. So they are also building a&nbsp;
satellite to accompany the factory in space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:48.640
and serve as a command and control module.&nbsp;
Lastly, once the product is actually finished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:54.480
you have to get it back down to earth, and that’s&nbsp;
no easy task. Atmospheric re-entry is an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:59.760
risky maneuver and by far the hardest part of&nbsp;
getting anything from space home safely. 
&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.960
“We’re regulated by the FAA because we’re&nbsp;
re-entering over airspace. If we miss the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:09.040
atmosphere even by three or four seconds, you're&nbsp;
talking about rather than landing in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.360
States, you’re accidentally landing in Europe.”
This really illustrates how aggressive Delian’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:18.080
plan is for Varda. There are a few other companies&nbsp;
that are working on space manufacturing currently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:23.120
but they mostly focus on developing the factory&nbsp;
exclusively and then partner with NASA to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:28.000
everything to the space station and back. 
The scale of the reentry problem really can’t be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:33.440
understated. In order to stay in orbit, anything&nbsp;
in space needs to be traveling incredibly fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:38.640
A commercial airliner typically flies at around&nbsp;
500 miles per hour. But the international space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:45.680
station is traveling at 17 thousand miles&nbsp;
per hour. The deceleration that occurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:50.640
during reentry is extremely powerful,&nbsp;
and anything Varda makes in space will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.120
have to be able to make it through reentry&nbsp;
without getting damaged in the process. 
&nbsp;

00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:01.040
This part of the equation is so critical to&nbsp;
Varda’s success that Delian even recruited a CEO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:06.400
with experience specifically in reentry.
“But I realized that for the CEO position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:10.720
I wanted somebody that had been at SpaceX and&nbsp;
specifically on Dragon in particular because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:14.480
have a reentry problem that we're trying to solve&nbsp;
and the Dragon is basically the only commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:18.720
reentry vehicle that's ever been built.” 
Varda is clearly a moonshot type of company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:24.560
but Delian is placing his bets very carefully.&nbsp;
SpaceX is top notch in terms of rockets, so no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:29.760
need to reengineer things there. But no one has&nbsp;
figured out how to solve reentry at the industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:35.040
scale Varda is aiming for, not even NASA.
NASA is an amazing organization, but their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:42.000
budget is just $24 billion dollars per year. For&nbsp;
comparison, Walmart spends that much money every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:48.240
3 weeks. While partnering with government research&nbsp;
teams can work in some cases, it’s not ideal for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:53.840
scaling an entirely new industry. For Varda to&nbsp;
be truly successful, they need to be launching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.920
dozens of factories to space every month.
“Yeah, I mean with Varda the initial market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:01.440
that we’re going after, if we were to eventually&nbsp;
satisfy the entire market that we’re going after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.800
we would have multiple of our reentry&nbsp;
capsules coming down per day and we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:10.960
be buying a SpaceX rocket every other day.”
Okay, so clearly Varda has ambitious goals, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:16.800
exactly how big of a market is space manufacturing&nbsp;
anyway? Well, it’s currently a rounding error,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:22.720
but the global space industry is valued around&nbsp;
$420 billion dollars and that’s growing really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:28.080
rapidly. Just last year investors poured nearly&nbsp;
$10 billion dollars into space companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:32.800
Each with slightly different approaches. 
Companies like Hadrian and Relativity are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:38.960
developing products on Earth that are meant for&nbsp;
space. And a company called “Made in Space” is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:44.000
you guessed it, making things in space.&nbsp;
But everything Made in Space produces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.520
is meant to stay in space, not come down&nbsp;
to Earth. That’s where Varda is unique.
&nbsp;

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:54.560
When it comes to the growth of the space industry,&nbsp;
we’re seeing steady progress start to compound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:59.680
in all sorts of important metrics. The cost of&nbsp;
launching a satellite is falling dramatically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:02.800
The time between launches is&nbsp;
getting shorter and shorter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:07.920
And even the number of humans who have been to&nbsp;
space is increasing faster than ever before.
&nbsp;

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:12.960
When Jeff Bezos returned to Earth after his&nbsp;
trip on Blue Origin’s New Shepard rocket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:17.120
he had some really poignant words about&nbsp;
the importance of space manufacturing:
&nbsp;

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:20.480
"We need to take all heavy&nbsp;
industry, all polluting industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:25.520
and move it into space. And keep Earth as this&nbsp;
beautiful gem of a planet that it is. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

